To make Bootstrap tables sortable alphanumerically without using jQuery or other plugins, you can use the HTML5 Drag and Drop API. Here’s a step-by-step guide:

1. **HTML Structure**: Ensure your table has a structure that supports sorting. Each header cell (`<th>`) should have a unique identifier or class to indicate it is sortable.

2. **CSS Styling**: Add some CSS to style the headers and provide visual feedback when they are being dragged or sorted.

3. **JavaScript Implementation**: Use the HTML5 Drag and Drop API to handle the sorting logic. Here is a basic example:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <style>
        .sortable th {
            cursor: pointer;
            user-select: none;
        }

        .sortable th:hover {
            background-color: #f0f0f0;
        }

        .sortable th.dragging {
            background-color: #ddd;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <table class="sortable">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th draggable="true">Name</th>
                <th draggable="true">Age</th>
                <th draggable="true">City</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>John</td>
                <td>25</td>
                <td>New York</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Jane</td>
                <td>30</td>
                <td>Los Angeles</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Bob</td>
                <td>22</td>
                <td>Chicago</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <script>
        const table = document.querySelector('.sortable');
        const headers = table.querySelectorAll('th[draggable="true"]');
        const rows = table.querySelectorAll('tbody tr');

        headers.forEach(header => {
            header.addEventListener('dragstart', (e) => {
                e.dataTransfer.setData('text/plain', header.id);
                header.classList.add('dragging');
            });

            header.addEventListener('dragend', () => {
                header.classList.remove('dragging');
            });
        });

        rows.forEach(row => {
            row.addEventListener('dragover', (e) => {
                e.preventDefault();
            });

            row.addEventListener('drop', (e) => {
                e.preventDefault();
                const draggedHeader = document.querySelector('.dragging');
                const newOrder = Array.from(headers).map(h => h.id);
                const currentIndex = newOrder.indexOf(draggedHeader.id);
                const newIndex = Array.from(rows).indexOf(e.target);

                if (currentIndex !== newIndex) {
                    const rowToMove = rows[currentIndex];
                    rows.splice(currentIndex, 1);
                    rows.splice(newIndex, 0, rowToMove);
                }
            });
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code sets up a basic sortable table where you can drag and drop rows to reorder them. The headers are marked as draggable, and the rows handle the drop events to reorder the table.

What is the purpose of the `draggable` attribute in the table headers?

The `draggable` attribute in the table headers indicates that the header is draggable. This allows users to click and drag the header to initiate a sort operation. When a header is dragged, the browser triggers the drag and drop events, which can be handled by JavaScript to perform the sorting logic.

